  "final_text": "I finished all four steps, and I read each result back from Kibana to confirm it.\n\n**1. Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Flights'** (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`). Its time field is **`timestamp`**. Its fields are:\n- **Text:** `Carrier`, `Dest`, `DestAirportID`, `DestCityName`, `DestCountry`, `DestRegion`, `DestWeather`, `FlightDelayType`, `FlightNum`, `FlightTimeHour`, `Origin`, `OriginAirportID`, `OriginCityName`, `OriginCountry`, `OriginRegion`, `OriginWeather`\n- **Numbers:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n- **True/false:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`\n- **Locations:** `DestLocation`, `OriginLocation`\n- **Date:** `timestamp`\n- Plus the standard metadata fields (`_id`, `_index`, `_score`, `_source`, `_ignored`).\n\n**2. Dashboard 'S4-22b746dc Flights'** (id `8c6642fd-cec6-4cd7-a37a-86d6706fcf9a`) has exactly two panels, both using the data view above:\n- \"Flights over time\": a line chart of the flight count over `timestamp`.\n- A bar chart of the flight count for the top 10 values of `Carrier`.\n\n**3. Alert rule 'S4-22b746dc Cancelled flights'** (id `ab57ad94-2978-4d5d-98f9-9bad05b17601`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. It runs every minute, matches flights where `Cancelled` is true, and fires when more than 10 of them fall within the last 5 minutes. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to turn it on.\n\n**4. Edit and read-back:** The carrier panel's title is now \"Flights by carrier\", and the dashboard still has only those two panels. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true` with the 1-minute check and the \"more than 10 in 5 minutes\" condition unchanged.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
